le bergerac took the  helm  a year later revlon sales reached nearly  1 billion  although it was still second in sales  behind avon products with this  door-to-door distribution  the cosmetics conglomerate kept pace  with changing trends as more women  entered the workforce  marketing its products to on-the-go  career-driven women  now the world belongs to charlie by  revlon  but by the early 1980s sales had  flattened  and in a decade marked by hostile  takeovers one of the top corporate  raiders had revlon in his sights  businessman ron perlman initiated a  hostile takeover of revlon in 1985.  the takeover bid dominated headlines  detailing the struggle between pearlman  and bergerac for control of the company  he said to me very proudly that  he hunted lions and tigers so  i was not a threat to him ultimately  pearlman prevailed  becoming chairman of revlon's board and  controlling shareholder  pearlman enlisted famed fashion  photographer richard avedon to shoot the  unforgettable ad campaign bringing top  supermodels like christy turlington  and cindy crawford to be the faces of  revlon at first blush  revlon appeared poised to end the  century on a high note  however its finances were telling a  different story  perlman had used junk bonds to finance  the takeover  it was assumed he would sell revlon's  non-beauty businesses to pay down this  debt  instead he turned his focus to a failed  bid to take over gillette  and this came at a time when competition  was heating up  procter gamble acquired several makeup  brands snatching up a third of the  cosmetics market by 1990 compared to  revlon's share of just over 20 percent  at the same time revlon was stretched  thin it was carrying nine upscale  product lines with nearly 3 000  individual products  but women were wearing less and less  makeup as they moved away from the  bright colors of the 80s to more muted  natural looks the company launched its  hit colorstay non-transferable lipstick  in 1995  and went public in 1996. however much of  revlon's pre-tax profits were going to  service its 1.3 billion dollars of debt  late in the century it turned into a  company that was just a little bit  behind the trends  um you know things started fashion  started shifting away from these bright  colors  and revlon didn't adapt to that change  uh it didn't it you know it caught on  somewhat late to the shift toward  skin care and skin health and was still  really  selling kind of these um you know these  color cosmetics that were  changing by 2003 revlon had experienced  16 straight quarters of losses  and by 2007 its share of the cosmetics  market had fallen to  11 in mass-market retailers  despite this perlman continued to keep  the company afloat  with injections of cash in a revolving  door of ceos  mr perlman he said uh even this was more  than a decade ago in an interview  with the wall street journal he said you  know for good or bad i'm defined by  revlon  he led rescues he just bailed the  company out over and over again as it  continued to struggle  revlon tried to recapture part of the  market with high profile acquisitions of  calmer group in elizabeth arden  but by 2017 its market share had  dwindled to around six percent  by then revlon was not only facing stiff  competition from the likes of procter  and gamble and l'oreal  but from startups with serious star  power  celebrity-backed makeup lines like kylie  jenner's kylie cosmetics  had burst onto the scene you know  company whether it's revlon whether it's  covergirl these are companies that have  massive advertising budgets models  tv commercials and you know with the  with the rise of social media we're  seeing brands that are driven by  you know we influencers by you know  kylie jenn jenner who has  um you know a cosmetics company that has  200 million  dollars in annual sales even though you  know there's only less than a dozen  people who work at her company  and she doesn't spend money on  advertising because she just has so many  instagram followers  in 2018 perlman provided revlon with a  financial lifeline  and installed his daughter deborah as  ceo the company's first  female chief executive through much of  2020  widespread closures and bankruptcies  among retailers salons and spas  crimp sales of beauty products the main  thing is that  our lips are covered when we wear masks  so lipstick sales have  have fallen off there used to be a term  in the industry called the lipstick and  index and how much women were spending  on makeup kind of as a proxy for the  economy and he said we can't really use  the lipstick index anymore because  nobody can see our lips the dip couldn't  have come  at a worse time for revlon with a 343  million dollar bond payment loaming  revlon refinanced its debt to avoid  bankruptcy  if you look at kind of some of the  analysts and some of the industry  experts they question whether this  deal to safe revlon has just kicked the  can down the road  um certainly old brands brands that are  struggling have  revived themselves before i would say  revlon has a tougher road and it's an  entire company not  a brand but it's hard you know it's hard  to say where they're going to end up  where revlon ends up will depend on how  it adapts to the modern beauty landscape  and if it can inject a little color into  an otherwise bleak outlook
 

